01:17:23.890 --> 01:17:26.190
It's the length of the first
times the length of the second

01:17:26.190 --> 01:17:29.090
times the cosine of the angle
between them.

01:17:29.090 --> 01:17:30.890
But this is not a different dot
product.

01:17:30.890 --> 01:17:32.390
This is

01:17:34.650 --> 01:17:39.900
equal to the same dot product we
saw before.

01:17:45.990 --> 01:17:47.910
This new one that we found,

01:17:47.910 --> 01:17:51.660
I might call the geometric
definition,

01:17:54.230 --> 01:17:58.730
and this original one I might
call the algebraic definition.

01:18:00.210 --> 01:18:01.330
Now you might say, well,

01:18:01.330 --> 01:18:04.830
when would I ever know the angle
between two vectors?

01:18:04.830 --> 01:18:07.330
The answer is you wouldn't
already know the angle.

01:18:07.330 --> 01:18:10.650
What this allows you to do is
compute the angle between two

01:18:10.650 --> 01:18:12.910
vectors, or at least the cosine
of the angle between two

01:18:12.910 --> 01:18:13.910
vectors.

01:18:14.090 --> 01:18:17.130
And what that gives us is a way
of measuring

01:18:17.130 --> 01:18:22.890
how similar two vectors are even
when they're in higher than

01:18:22.890 --> 01:18:23.690
R3,

01:18:23.690 --> 01:18:25.950
or they have more than three
components.

01:18:25.950 --> 01:18:29.110
All of these formulas work, even
if the vectors have 74 things in

01:18:29.110 --> 01:18:30.110
them.

01:18:32.870 --> 01:18:35.650
If you took your two vectors in
R74

01:18:35.650 --> 01:18:38.370
and used the algebraic
definition to get their dot

01:18:38.370 --> 01:18:42.120
product, and you found their
lengths as well,

01:18:42.630 --> 01:18:45.130
well, then you could solve for
the cosine of the angle between

01:18:45.130 --> 01:18:46.130
them.

01:18:46.270 --> 01:18:49.170
And what is the cosine of an
angle?

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:53.670
What's the max and min that
could be?

01:18:53.750 --> 01:18:54.650
Yeah,

01:18:54.650 --> 01:18:55.650
great.

01:18:56.410 --> 01:18:58.470
Negative 1 and 1.
Yeah, it's between negative 1

01:18:58.470 --> 01:18:59.250
and 1.

01:18:59.250 --> 01:19:01.530
If the cosine of an angle is 1,

01:19:01.530 --> 01:19:04.310
that means the vectors point in
the exact same direction.

01:19:04.310 --> 01:19:08.190
The theta between them is 0.

01:19:08.190 --> 01:19:11.010
If they point in exact opposite
directions,

01:19:11.010 --> 01:19:16.970
well then the theta is 180 and
the cosine of theta

01:19:16.970 --> 01:19:17.870
is negative one.

01:19:17.870 --> 01:19:20.350
That's why when they point in
opposite directions,

01:19:20.350 --> 01:19:22.330
the dot product is negative.

01:19:22.330 --> 01:19:24.150
And when they're perpendicular,

01:19:24.150 --> 01:19:28.450
what's the cosine of 90 degrees?

01:19:28.450 --> 01:19:29.450
Zero.

01:19:30.510 --> 01:19:33.290
So all of that behavior is
coming from this equivalence.

01:19:33.290 --> 01:19:37.550
The last thing I'll write here
is that if you write out u dot v

01:19:37.550 --> 01:19:42.800
equals the length of u length of
v cos theta

01:19:43.030 --> 01:19:47.990
You can solve for just the cos
theta, which is u dot v

01:19:47.990 --> 01:19:50.370
over the length of u

01:19:50.370 --> 01:19:53.750
Times the length of v and
sometimes this is called the

01:19:53.750 --> 01:19:54.750
cosine

01:19:56.590 --> 01:19:57.590
Similarity

01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:04.790
And for the reasons we just
discussed, because the cosine of

01:20:04.790 --> 01:20:07.450
an angle is between negative one
and one,

01:20:07.450 --> 01:20:08.770
so is this.
And this gives us a way of

01:20:08.770 --> 01:20:11.690
measuring the similarity of two
vectors with any number of

01:20:11.690 --> 01:20:12.690
things in them.

